const e=JSON.parse(`[{"id":"OO_E6_001","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"five_sentences","sentences":{"1":"Although dengue is more lethal, some say chikungunya hurts more and lasts longer.","2":"The disease, which is spread by mosquitoes and is common in Africa and Asia has now come to the Caribbean.","3":"Those diagnosed with chikungunya fever may not care to know that its name, an East African word, means 'that which bends up', a reference to the contorted posture of sufferers.","4":"Dengue is already endemic along the Texas-Mexico border, and this strain of chikungunya is carried by the Aedes aegypti mosquito, which also deals in dengue and prefers tropical regions.","5":"The pain, felt mostly in the joints, makes chikungunya similar to dengue fever."},"correct_sequence":["3","5","1","2"],"odd_sentence":"4","odd_sentence_error":"introduces_new_concept","odd_sentence_reason":"Talks about dengue being endemic along the Texas-Mexico border and a specific strain of chikungunya carried by a specific mosquito — introduces geographic and strain-specific details not related to the paragraph's general discussion of chikungunya.","wrong_pick_errors":{"1":"missed_thematic_link","2":"misread_scope","3":"missed_pronoun_link","5":"missed_connector_link"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"The paragraph talks about chikungunya, starting from the meaning of the name to its comparison with dengue and talks about its prevalence.\\nSentence 3 is the best sentence to begin with because it introduces the disease, and the meaning of its name.\\nSentence 5 is the second sentence because it uses the noun the pain to imply the pain endured by the sufferers mentioned in the earlier sentence.\\nSentence 1 should follow sentence 5 because the former extends the comparison given in the latter sentence.\\nSentence 2 is the last sentence because it talks about the general prevalence of the disease.\\nThus, the correct sequence is 3512.\\nSentence 4 is the odd sentence because it talks about dengue and one particular strain of chikungunya does not belong to the paragraph that talks about the disease in a general way.\\nThus, the correct option is Sentence 4. Ans : (4)","time_benchmark_seconds":120,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Each of the following questions presents 5 statements of which 4, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_002","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"five_sentences","sentences":{"1":"And yet selfies continue to explode in number, reproducing exponentially, filling up server farms and data plans and camera rolls.","2":"It's that everyone who posts a selfie must be getting something out of it, something so life-affirming that it makes the harshness endurable.","3":"No one posts a picture to social media without anticipating the mockery, it will receive, either in the form of direct comments or indirect hatred from the wider media.","4":"Selfies are not inherently political acts, but these resonant, addictive unregulated images are another manifestation of this growing distrust of the mainstream and the swelling desire by many individuals to reclaim their own narratives now that they have the virtual microphone.","5":"It's not just that a cultural faucet, once turned on can never be turned off."},"correct_sequence":["3","1","5","2"],"odd_sentence":"4","odd_sentence_error":"shifts_focus","odd_sentence_reason":"Talks about selfies as manifestations of distrust of the mainstream and desire to reclaim narratives — a political/sociological angle not related to the main idea about why people post selfies despite possible mockery.","wrong_pick_errors":{"1":"missed_connector_link","2":"misread_scope","3":"missed_thematic_link","5":"missed_mandatory_pair"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"When we go through the sentences, we understand that the paragraph is about the popularity of selfies, despite hatred that might be engendered.\\nSentence 3 is the first sentence because it mentions people's posting pictures on social media despite possible unfavorable comments.\\nSentence 1 is the second sentence because it uses yet to show the contrast between two elements; one, people posting selfies despite adverse comments and two, rapid expansion of selfie-posting.\\nSentence 5 is the third sentence because it further expands the selfie posting habit.\\nSentence 2 is the concluding sentence because it explains why people post selfies.\\nThus, the correct sequence is 3152.\\nSentence 4 is the odd sentence because it talks about another idea which is not related to the main idea of the paragraph.\\nThus, the odd sentence is Sentence 4. Ans : (4)","time_benchmark_seconds":120,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Each of the following questions presents 5 statements of which 4, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_003","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"five_sentences","sentences":{"1":"In most of the developing countries the work opportunities available for the poor are so restricted that they cannot work their way out of misery.","2":"Many of them have no land and no prospect of ever getting any.","3":"Rural unemployment produces mass migration into cities, leading to a rate of urban growth which would tax the resources of even the richest societies.","4":"They are underemployed or totally unemployed and when they do find occasional work their productivity is exceedingly low.","5":"There is no hope for them in the rural areas and hence they drift into the big cities."},"correct_sequence":["1","4","2","5"],"odd_sentence":"3","odd_sentence_error":"shifts_focus","odd_sentence_reason":"Talks about the stress caused on urban resources due to mass migration — a consequence of migration not directly related to the paragraph's main idea of how rural unemployment leads to migration.","wrong_pick_errors":{"1":"missed_thematic_link","2":"missed_pronoun_link","4":"missed_pronoun_link","5":"missed_thematic_link"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"The main idea of the paragraph is to describe how rural unemployment results in migration to urban areas.\\nSentence 1 is the first sentence because it introduces the topic. We can note that all other sentences have cross-references.\\nSentence 4 is the second sentence because it describes the work limitations, a concept presented in the earlier sentence.\\nSentence 2 is the third sentence because it adds one more reason for the rural unemployment.\\nSentence 5 is the last sentence because it explains how the rural unemployment results in migration to urban areas.\\nThus, the proper sequence is 1425.\\nSentence 3 is odd because it talks about the stress caused on resources due to mass migration, a concept that is not directly related to the main idea of the paragraph.\\nThus, the odd sentence is Sentence 3. Ans : (3)","time_benchmark_seconds":120,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Each of the following questions presents 5 statements of which 4, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_004","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"five_sentences","sentences":{"1":"All traditional philosophy is an attempt to create an orderly system of ideas by which to live and to interpret the world.","2":"It is difficult to bear the resultant feeling of emptiness and the vacuum of our minds may only too easily be filled by some big, fantastic notion, political or otherwise which suddenly seems to illumine everything and to give meaning and purpose to our existence.","3":"The way in which we experience and interpret the world depends very much on the kind of ideas that fill our minds.","4":"If they are mainly small, weak, superficial and incoherent, life will appear insipid uninteresting, petty and chaotic.","5":"It needs no emphasis that herein lies one of the great dangers of our time."},"correct_sequence":["3","4","2","5"],"odd_sentence":"1","odd_sentence_error":"introduces_new_concept","odd_sentence_reason":"Talks about traditional philosophy as a system of ideas — introduces philosophies which are not mentioned in any other sentence in the paragraph.","wrong_pick_errors":{"2":"missed_connector_link","3":"misread_scope","4":"missed_pronoun_link","5":"misread_scope"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"Going through the sentences, we understand that the main idea of the paragraph is the importance of ideas that fill our minds.\\nSentence 3 is the first sentence because of its introductory nature.\\nSentence 4 is the second sentence because it uses the pronoun they to refer to the ideas mentioned in the earlier sentence.\\nSentence 2 is the third sentence because it uses resultant feelings to give out the results of the insipid, uninteresting, petty and chaotic life talked about in the earlier sentence.\\nSentence 5 is the last sentence because it tells the implication of the negative ideas to the modern life and this way it concludes the paragraph.\\nThus, the correct sequence is 3425.\\nSentence 1 is the odd sentence because it talks about philosophies which are not yet mentioned in any context till now.\\nThus, the odd sentence is Sentence 1. Ans : (1)","time_benchmark_seconds":120,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Each of the following questions presents 5 statements of which 4, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_005","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"five_sentences","sentences":{"1":"Canaletto, Monet, Turner: some of the world's best known artists have tried to capture the Thames in paint.","2":"In gauzy greys and blues, thick with fog and luminescent with moonlight, they reveal themselves slowly after the eyes adjust, like the sharpening of night vision.","3":"His most profound vision came later in life, in his paintings and prints of the river at night.","4":"After decades of experimentation, Whistler had come to believe that art could exist for its own sake, a novel idea at the time.","5":"In the mid 19th century it was the turn of James Mc Neill Whistler an American painter, who saw the river as a rich vein through a changing city."},"correct_sequence":["1","5","3","2"],"odd_sentence":"4","odd_sentence_error":"shifts_focus","odd_sentence_reason":"Talks about the purpose of art — art for its own sake — which is not the major concept in the other sentences. The paragraph focuses on paintings of the Thames, not on the philosophy of art.","wrong_pick_errors":{"1":"missed_thematic_link","2":"missed_mandatory_pair","3":"missed_pronoun_link","5":"missed_pronoun_link"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"The paragraph talks about the evolution in the paintings of River Thames, starting with some well-known painters and discussing one painter in particular.\\nSentence 1 is the first sentence because it introduces some best painters who tried to capture the beauty of the Thames in painting.\\nSentence 5 is the second sentence because it talks about Whistler's turn in depicting the beauty of the River in painting. We need to identify the reference the river to refer to the Thames introduced in the first sentence.\\nSentence 3 and Sentence 2 are a fixed pair because the pronoun they in sentence 2 can logically refer to paintings and prints of the river at night, a phrase mentioned in Sentence 3.\\nThus, the correct sequence is 1532.\\nSentence 4 is the odd sentence because it cannot be linked with any of these statements. Moreover, it talks about the purpose of art, which is not the major concept in the other sentences.\\nThus, the odd sentence is Sentence 4. Ans : (4)","time_benchmark_seconds":120,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Each of the following questions presents 5 statements of which 4, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_006","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"five_sentences","sentences":{"1":"But in a recent study published in 'Scientific Reports', this is not true.","2":"The upshot is that elite universities do not, at least as far as physicists are concerned, add value to output.","3":"They believe perching on one of the topmost branches of the academic tree will also improve the quality of their work, by bringing them together with other geniuses with whom they can collaborate and who may help sport new ideas.","4":"But scholars covet such places for reasons beyond glory and gastronomy.","5":"Most academics would view a post at an elite University like Oxford or Harvard as the crowning achievement of a career bringing both accolades and access to better wine cellars."},"correct_sequence":["5","4","3","1"],"odd_sentence":"2","odd_sentence_error":"wrong_scope","odd_sentence_reason":"Talks about the quality of output of elite university physicists — the paragraph is about what attracts scholars to elite universities (prestige, personal calibre), not about quality of output.","wrong_pick_errors":{"1":"missed_connector_link","3":"missed_pronoun_link","4":"missed_connector_link","5":"misread_scope"},"difficulty":"hard","explanation":"The passage tries to explain the great prestige attached to teaching positions at places like Oxford. It further states that, contrary to popular belief, it is not the prestige and money that attract people to it, but a desire to improve their personal caliber.\\nSentence 5 is the first sentence because it introduces the prestige attached to such places. Moreover, it can be understood that all other statements do not make sense unless they are preceded by an earlier statement.\\nSentence 4 is the second sentence because it uses the noun such places to refer to places mentioned in the earlier sentence.\\nSentence 3 is the third sentence because it refers to the scholars mentioned in the earlier sentence.\\nSentence 1 is the last sentence because it contradicts with, by using the conjunction but, the information present in the third sentence.\\nThus, the proper sequence is 5431.\\nSentence 2 is the odd sentence because it talks about the quality of output, not of the faculty.\\nThus, the odd sentence is Sentence 2. Ans : (2)","time_benchmark_seconds":120,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Each of the following questions presents 5 statements of which 4, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_007","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"five_sentences","sentences":{"1":"This support helps us feel that what happens to us matters to other people.","2":"Our social relationships are a powerful source of meaningfulness, in good times and in bad.","3":"Ultimately, we care most about what other human beings are to us not whether the cosmos cares about us.","4":"We can and do care about our own lives and about those of our fellow human beings, even if the universe doesn't.","5":"The simple empathy and emotional support that people instinctively offer each other in times of tragedy and suffering is highly meaningful to the sufferer— even if only through consolation."},"correct_sequence":["2","5","1","3"],"odd_sentence":"4","odd_sentence_error":"shifts_focus","odd_sentence_reason":"States that we care about our own lives and those of others even if the universe does not — conveys a different idea about caring for life rather than about the meaningfulness of empathy and support from others.","wrong_pick_errors":{"1":"missed_connector_link","2":"misread_scope","3":"misread_scope","5":"missed_thematic_link"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"The paragraph explains that we tend to attach a lot of importance to the empathy and emotional support that we receive from others during bad times.\\nStatement 2: This statement offers an ideal introduction to the paragraph stating that our social relationships matter a lot in good times and in bad.\\nStatement 5: This statement complements what is stated in 2 by saying that the empathy and emotional support that a sufferer receives from others is highly meaningful.\\nStatement 1: This statement, which is linked to 5 by the word \\"support\\", carries forward what is stated in 5.\\nStatement 3: This statement sums up what is stated in 2,5 and 1 declaring that we ultimately care about what other humans think of us and not whether the cosmos cares about us.\\nTherefore, statements 2, 5, 1 and 3 form a sequence.\\nStatement 4, which states that we care about our own lives and those of others even if the universe does not, conveys a different idea and is not a part of the sequence 2, 5, 1, 3.\\nAns : (4)","time_benchmark_seconds":120,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Each of the following questions presents 5 statements of which 4, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_008","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"five_sentences","sentences":{"1":"Reasonable restrictions on the internet are legitimate on a country-by-country basis as there are varying interpretations of liberties due to cultural and socio-economic factors.","2":"It is therefore a discomfiting fact that despite the communitarian growth of the internet, the threats to freedom of expression on the medium, such as content takedowns, online surveillance and other forms of state control, have increased in the past year.","3":"The report is fairly comprehensive in its categorization of online freedom and curbs on it through different mechanism adopted by nation-states.","4":"The evolution of the internet from a forum for communication and commerce to a medium of free exchange of opinion and views has been so rapid that the nearly ubiquitous worldwide network of computers is now almost an extension of social life for many on the planet.","5":"A report on 'Freedom on the Net', released by Freedom House, the U.S-based, government funded organization, points to the overall decline in the internet freedom following a country-wise analysis of nation-states."},"correct_sequence":["4","2","5","3"],"odd_sentence":"1","odd_sentence_error":"too_general","odd_sentence_reason":"A general statement about reasonable restrictions being legitimate — cannot be linked to any of the other statements which are specifically about the decline of internet freedom documented in a specific report.","wrong_pick_errors":{"2":"missed_connector_link","3":"missed_mandatory_pair","4":"misread_scope","5":"missed_mandatory_pair"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"The paragraph talks about the spread of the internet and also about restrictions on internet freedom.\\nSentence 4 is the first sentence because it gives general information about the spread of the internet.\\nSentence 2 is the second sentence because it contrasts the spread of the internet (mentioned in the earlier sentence) and the threats to freedom of expression on the medium (mentioned in this sentence). The sentence uses the expression therefore a discomfiting fact to bring out this contrast.\\nSentence 5 is the next sentence because it corroborates the information in Sentence 2 by giving factual evidence.\\nSentence 3 is the next sentence because the expression the report logically refers to the report mentioned in the earlier sentence.\\nThus, the correct sequence is 4253.\\nSentence 1 is odd because it is more general and cannot be linked with any of these statements.\\nThus, the odd sentence is Sentence 1. Ans : (1)","time_benchmark_seconds":120,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Each of the following questions presents 5 statements of which 4, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_009","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"five_sentences","sentences":{"1":"The painful experiences throughout our lifetime occur against a background of genes that make us more or less sensitive to pain.","2":"Under normal circumstances, pain signals injury, and the natural response is to protect ourselves until we have recovered and the pain subsides.","3":"Pain is the single most common symptom reported when seeking medical attention.","4":"Unfortunately, people differ not only in their ability to detect, tolerate and respond to pain but also in how they report it and how they respond to various treatments.","5":"This makes it difficult to know how to effectively treat each patient."},"correct_sequence":["3","2","4","5"],"odd_sentence":"1","odd_sentence_error":"introduces_new_concept","odd_sentence_reason":"Talks about a genetic connection to our ability to respond to pain — a different idea that does not form part of the sequence about how individual differences in pain reporting make treatment difficult.","wrong_pick_errors":{"2":"missed_thematic_link","3":"misread_scope","4":"missed_connector_link","5":"missed_mandatory_pair"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"The passage explains that the way each individual responds to pain is different and goes on to state that the way people report pain and how they respond to various treatments is different thus making it difficult to offer treatment.\\nStatement 3: This statement introduces the paragraph stating that pain is the single most common symptom reported when seeking medical attention.\\nStatement 2: This statement follows 3 explaining how we try to protect ourselves from pain.\\nStatement 4: This statement carries the idea forward stating that people's ability to report pain and the way they respond to treatment is different.\\nStatement 5: This statement concludes the paragraph explaining the consequence of people not responding to treatment of pain.\\nTherefore, statements 3,2,4,5 form a sequence. Statement 1, which talks about a genetic connection to our ability to respond to pain, conveys a different idea and does not form a part of the given sequence. Ans : (1)","time_benchmark_seconds":120,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Each of the following questions presents 5 statements of which 4, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_010","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"five_sentences","sentences":{"1":"But what we overlook is that these beautiful creatures which are the flagship species for biodiversity conservation and indicators of a healthy ecosystem, are in danger of extinction.","2":"For generations, butterflies have been waging a deadly and long drawn-out war with their natural enemy, parasitoids who in their egg and larval stage, live in the tissue of the host which in this case is the butterfly, and feed on it.","3":"India is home to 1,800 species and subspecies that are increasingly being valued for aesthetic reasons.","4":"There are many factors that are detrimental to the conservation of butterflies, such as pesticide drifts, industrial pollution, deforestation, encroachment of natural habitat, lack of appropriate flora and even illegal butterfly trade.","5":"There is something magical about butterflies in different colours flitting around from one flower to another."},"correct_sequence":["5","3","1","2"],"odd_sentence":"4","odd_sentence_error":"shifts_focus","odd_sentence_reason":"Talks about factors detrimental to conservation of butterflies — a different angle not directly related to the paragraph's chain of ideas about butterflies being beautiful, undervalued and endangered.","wrong_pick_errors":{"1":"missed_connector_link","2":"missed_thematic_link","3":"missed_thematic_link","5":"misread_scope"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"The paragraph talks about butterflies and the reasons for their potential extinction.\\nSentence 5 is the first sentence because it introduces the subject – butterflies.\\nSentence 3 is the second sentence because it gives further reasons for the high regard butterflies enjoy.\\nSentence 1 is the third sentence because it brings out an alarming fact about butterflies (by using but) in contrast to the positive facts presented about them in the earlier sentences.\\nSentence 2 is the last sentence because it explains the danger of extinction mentioned in the earlier sentence.\\nThus, the correct order to form a coherent paragraph is 5312.\\nSentence 4 is the odd sentence because it talks about the factors which are detrimental for conservation.\\nThus, the odd sentence is Sentence 4. Ans : (4)","time_benchmark_seconds":120,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Each of the following questions presents 5 statements of which 4, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_011","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"five_sentences","sentences":{"1":"The wife of a Muslim scholar and teacher from Lucknow, Mrs. Ali was one of many British visitors to the court, ordinary men and women who were curious about the fort and the padishah within it.","2":"When Akbar Shah II took the throne in 1806, he had no army to lead, no territory to conquer and no city, let alone an empire, to rule.","3":"Yet when Mrs. Ali arrived in the emperor's presence, she took care to observe the expected courtesies.","4":"Late in Akbar Shah II's reign, an Englishwoman with the unusual name of Mrs. Meer Hassan Ali happened to make a visit to the Qila-i-Mualla.","5":"By this time, most of the palaces Shah Jahan had carefully built long ago were rundown; a visitor in 1824 reported seeing dirty floors, blocked water channels, broken furniture covered in pigeon droppings, and a garden in the sort of disorder that would have brought Babur to tears."},"correct_sequence":["4","1","5","3"],"odd_sentence":"2","odd_sentence_error":"shifts_focus","odd_sentence_reason":"Provides information about the conditions under which Akbar Shah II took the throne — shifts the focus from Mrs. Ali's visit to the historical context of the emperor's accession.","wrong_pick_errors":{"1":"missed_pronoun_link","3":"missed_connector_link","4":"misread_scope","5":"missed_thematic_link"},"difficulty":"hard","explanation":"Statement 4 introduces the topic: Englishwoman Mrs. Meer Hassan Ali visiting the Qila-i-Mualla.\\nStatement 1 gives further information about Mrs. Ali and mentions she was among the many British individuals curious about the palace and the ruler (padishah).\\nStatement 5 details the bleak condition of most palaces Shah Jahan had built.\\nStatement 3 follows as it highlights how despite the poor condition of the fort, Mrs. Ali ensured she observed the required courtesies to be followed when meeting the emperor.\\nStatements 4, 1, 5, and 3 form a logically coherent paragraph. While statement 2 also provides relevant information about the conditions under which Akbar Shah II took the throne, it shifts the focus from Mrs. Ali's visit.\\nTherefore, statement 2 is the odd man out. Ans: (2)","time_benchmark_seconds":120,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Each of the following questions presents 5 statements of which 4, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_012","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"five_sentences","sentences":{"1":"Each physical exercise causes a delayed expenditure of energy, which is added to the expenditure during the activity.","2":"The calorie expenditure associated with any activity is called the 'metabolic cost' and corresponds to the energy consumed by our organs to cover a given distance.","3":"When running, part of the energy expended is used to move our bodies upward rather than forward.","4":"When we run, the distance that our body moves up and down is greater than when we walk, and to produce this vertical movement, the muscles of the lower limbs have to generate more force, consuming more energy.","5":"Perhaps not surprisingly, running consumes more energy than walking for the same distance covered."},"correct_sequence":["2","5","4","3"],"odd_sentence":"1","odd_sentence_error":"introduces_new_concept","odd_sentence_reason":"Discusses delayed energy expenditure — a different aspect of energy expenditure not discussed in the paragraph which focuses on why running consumes more energy than walking.","wrong_pick_errors":{"2":"missed_thematic_link","3":"missed_mandatory_pair","4":"missed_thematic_link","5":"missed_connector_link"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"Statement 2 goes first as it introduces 'energy or calorie expenditure' during activities.\\nStatement 5 follows as it becomes more specific about running consuming more energy than walking for the same distance covered.\\nStatement 4 explains why running leads to higher energy expenditure.\\nStatement 3 ties up the paragraph by concluding that our bodies use part of the energy we expend while running to move our bodies upward instead of forward.\\nStatements 2, 5, 4, and 3 flow in a logically coherent manner.\\nStatement 1 discusses another aspect of energy expenditure (delayed energy expenditure), so it's the odd man out\\nAns: (1)","time_benchmark_seconds":120,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Each of the following questions presents 5 statements of which 4, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_013","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"five_sentences","sentences":{"1":"Through double taxation avoidance agreements, foreign entities can establish funds in locations outside their home countries to take advantage of lower taxes.","2":"As an isolated country located in the south-western Indian Ocean, Mauritius has linked itself to global financial sectors by easing the flow of capital into and out of its economy.","3":"The Mauritian government has previously found ways to diversify its economy during times of crisis.","4":"It has signed double taxation avoidance agreements with other countries, and its capital gains taxes are attractively low.","5":"For the last two decades, the economic growth of Mauritius has depended heavily on its offshore sector — the provision of financial services by banks to foreign firms."},"correct_sequence":["5","2","4","1"],"odd_sentence":"3","odd_sentence_error":"shifts_focus","odd_sentence_reason":"Talks about the Mauritian government finding ways to diversify its economy during times of crisis — despite discussing the Mauritian economy, it doesn't contribute to the flow of the paragraph about Mauritius's global financial integration.","wrong_pick_errors":{"1":"missed_mandatory_pair","2":"missed_thematic_link","4":"missed_pronoun_link","5":"misread_scope"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"Statement 5 begins the discussion about the economic growth of Mauritius heavily depending on its banks providing financial services to foreign firms.\\nStatement 2 further states how Mauritius has become a part of the global financial sectors by allowing a smooth flow of funds into and out of its economy.\\nStatement 4 becomes more specific about Mauritius signing double taxation avoidance agreements with other countries as one of the steps to become a part of the global economy.\\nStatement 1 explains how double taxation agreements benefit foreign entities.\\nStatements 5, 2, 4, and 1 make a logically coherent paragraph. Statement 3 is the odd man out because despite discussing the Mauritian government and economy, it doesn't contribute much to maintaining the flow of the paragraph. Ans: (3)","time_benchmark_seconds":120,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Each of the following questions presents 5 statements of which 4, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_014","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"five_sentences","sentences":{"1":"Towards the end of the movie Modern Times, the Tramp is eating breakfast with his love interest, known as the Gamin, herself a vagrant and an orphan, in a makeshift home she put up while the Tramp was in jail.","2":"The less the capitalist can pay the laborer, the more efficiently he can make him work, and the degree to which he can maximize the amount of time he works while in the factory, is advantageous to the capitalist.","3":"It's the first good breakfast either of them have had in a while — bacon, thick slices of bread, coffee — but right when he sits down to enjoy it, he sees a headline in the paper: 'Factories Reopen! Men to be Put to Work.'","4":"Immediately, he jumps up and rushes out to the factory, where he is part of a mob of other men trying to get work.","5":"In this society, when you're not employed, you must spend all of your time trying to find work — in this way, even when you're out of the control of a specific capitalist, you're still in the clutches of the system."},"correct_sequence":["1","3","4","5"],"odd_sentence":"2","odd_sentence_error":"shifts_focus","odd_sentence_reason":"Mentions how capitalists control labor through time and money — this context is about unemployment and the compulsion to seek work, and the capitalist-labor dynamic in sentence 2 cannot be connected to the immediate situation described.","wrong_pick_errors":{"1":"misread_scope","3":"missed_mandatory_pair","4":"missed_mandatory_pair","5":"misread_scope"},"difficulty":"hard","explanation":"It is easy to envisage that sentences 1,3,4 make a triplet in this seemingly complicated paragraph set. Sentence 1 mentions breakfast, and sentence 3 qualifies the breakfast as good. However, the breakfast is interrupted in sentence 3 with a notice on vacancies, which prompts the Tramp (Charlie Chaplin) to rush to join the mob of other (possibly broken-down) tramps outside the factory gates in sentence 4. But what is 134 trying to prove? Sentence 5 – that even when you're not employed, you must spend all of your time trying to find work, or people would make it their job to remind you that you are out of work and should make all efforts to find a job as soon as you possibly can, instead of letting a man relax and spend his own free time doing what he likes.\\nSentence 2 is the odd one out. It mentions how capitalists control labor through time and money, but the given context is largely on unemployment, and the factors given in sentence 2 have to be connected to the immediate situation, to be at all relevant. Ans: (2)","time_benchmark_seconds":150,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Each of the following questions presents 5 statements of which 4, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_015","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"five_sentences","sentences":{"1":"This trend was accelerated by war.","2":"Pizza was slow to move out of Naples, where it originated.","3":"From the 1930s onwards, a growing number of Neapolitans moved northwards in search of work, taking their cuisine with them.","4":"From the 1950s onwards, the rapid pace of economic and technological change in the far west transformed the pizza even more radically.","5":"The initial spur was provided by migration."},"correct_sequence":["2","5","3","1"],"odd_sentence":"4","odd_sentence_error":"introduces_new_concept","odd_sentence_reason":"Discusses how economic and technological change in the far west transformed pizza more radically — the connection of what made pizza transform radically in the far west is missing from the paragraph which focuses on migration within Italy.","wrong_pick_errors":{"1":"missed_thematic_link","2":"misread_scope","3":"missed_mandatory_pair","5":"missed_mandatory_pair"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"Sentences 1 and 5 cannot begin the paragraph as they need prior reference. Between sentences 2, 3 and 4, the foremost is a more suitable beginning to the passage. After sentence 2, sentence 5 follows to explain the situation in Naples where pizza first began moving out. Sentence 3 then coherently flows next giving more insight into when pizza started becoming popular through the efforts of the Neapolitans. The trend referred to in sentence 1 is the trend of moving northwards in search of work, which makes this sentence the next in order after sentence 3. Sentence 4 is out of context here as the connection of how what made pizza transform more radically in the far west is missing. Ans: (4)","time_benchmark_seconds":120,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Each of the following questions presents 5 statements of which 4, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_016","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"five_sentences","sentences":{"1":"Regulation is largely nonexistent, it may be difficult for governments to clamp down on fake news content, for fear of running afoul of free-speech protections.","2":"It's infeasible to deal quickly with the sheer number of such sites, you spot one [site], you shut it down, and there's another one created someplace else.","3":"Not all sources are equally credible, just because something claims to be a news site doesn't mean that they actually have a journalist … producing content.","4":"People should watch for clues in articles, red flags such as really odd grammar or errors in sentence construction.","5":"That leaves it to social media companies, which haven't done a good job so far."},"correct_sequence":["3","1","5","2"],"odd_sentence":"4","odd_sentence_error":"wrong_scope","odd_sentence_reason":"A prescription for what people should do — helpful but unlikely to work in the out-of-control fake news scenario described in the paragraph, and cannot be linked to the chain about regulation and social media companies.","wrong_pick_errors":{"1":"missed_mandatory_pair","2":"missed_mandatory_pair","3":"misread_scope","5":"missed_pronoun_link"},"difficulty":"hard","explanation":"Only 4 can begin the paragraph. 1, 2, 4, and 5 lack references – '(what) regulation(?)', 'such sites', ''(why) clues(?), 'leaves it (?)' respectively. 1 and 5 make a mandatory pair – 'it' in 5 refers to 'regulation' in 1. 5 and 2 make a mandatory pair, 2 explains why 'social media companies' in 5 'haven't done a good job so far' – because of the 'sheer number of such sites', you're never going to fully catch up with them. Thus, we have 1,5, 2,naturally following 3. The paragraph is 3152. 4 is a prescription, it's helpful, but unlikely to work in such an out-of-control scenario. Ans: (4)","time_benchmark_seconds":150,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Each of the following questions presents 5 statements of which 4, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_017","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"five_sentences","sentences":{"1":"'There's a lot of comedians that feel that you should be able to say whatever you want because you're a comedian. I don't believe in that,' she said.","2":"Ultimately, nobody should ever assault someone at work, she said.","3":"But Merrill Davis thinks comedians have to use their 'emotional intelligence'.","4":"'I think there's a delicate balance between an intelligent comedian knowing the audience, knowing the room.'","5":"The issue of 'cancel culture' has been in the spotlight for a number of years now, with some comedians saying a fear of offending - and the repercussions that follow - is killing comedy."},"correct_sequence":["5","3","1","4"],"odd_sentence":"2","odd_sentence_error":"introduces_new_concept","odd_sentence_reason":"Mentions a comedian being assaulted at work — a concern that has not been raised elsewhere in the passage which focuses on cancel culture and emotional intelligence in comedy.","wrong_pick_errors":{"1":"missed_pronoun_link","3":"missed_connector_link","4":"missed_mandatory_pair","5":"misread_scope"},"difficulty":"hard","explanation":"Only 5 can begin the paragraph, because 1 and 2 have the 'she said' scenario. 3 begins with 'but', indicating continuity. 4 itself is a quote without a source. 5 mentions that cancel culture is killing comedy, and only 3 can follow, since 1, 2, and 4 are quotes, without a revealed identity. 1 follows, stating what Merrill Davis feels is a wrong assumption that 'comedians' in 5 make. Merrill Davis then goes forth to make her case of the (emotionally) intelligent comedian in 4. 2 is out of context, as it mentions a comedian being assaulted, a concern that has not been raised elsewhere in the passage.\\nThe paragraph is 5314. Ans: (2)","time_benchmark_seconds":150,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Each of the following questions presents 5 statements of which 4, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_018","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"five_sentences","sentences":{"1":"According to respondents, the most crucial strategies to encourage preprinting are integrating preprinting into journal submission workflows and providing recognition for posting preprints.","2":"The adoption of preprinting is much stronger in physics and astronomy as well as in mathematics and computer science than in other research areas.","3":"There are also researchers that have more fundamental concerns, for instance because they fear that sharing results before peer review may be harmful.","4":"Low reliability and credibility of preprints, sharing results before peer review and premature media coverage are the most significant concerns about preprinting, emphasized in particular by respondents in the life and health sciences.","5":"Respondents identified free accessibility of preprints and acceleration of research communication as the most important benefits of preprinting."},"correct_sequence":["2","5","4","1"],"odd_sentence":"3","odd_sentence_error":"redundant","odd_sentence_reason":"Repeats the concern about sharing results before peer review may be harmful — this concern is already raised in sentence 4, making sentence 3 redundant.","wrong_pick_errors":{"1":"misread_scope","2":"misread_scope","4":"missed_thematic_link","5":"missed_mandatory_pair"},"difficulty":"hard","explanation":"Except 3, all the other sentences refer to 'preprints' – 4 and 5, and 'preprinting' – 1 and 2. However, in a large sense, preprints are introduced in 2, refer to 'adoption'. It is logical to follow with 5 – 'the most important benefits of preprinting'. 'Concerns' logically follow in 4. 1 looks at how the concerns raised in 4 can be resolved in a larger sense. It is not difficult to note that 3 is the odd one out – it repeats 'sharing results before peer review may be harmful', which is a concern raised in 4 anyway. The paragraph is 2541. And the odd one out is 3. Ans: (3)","time_benchmark_seconds":150,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Each of the following questions presents 5 statements of which 4, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_019","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"five_sentences","sentences":{"1":"There are striking examples of the value of having relatively unstructured, unpressured time to create and develop new ideas.","2":"Darwin spent decades reading scientific literature, making voyages on the HMS Beagle to the Galápagos and other exotic destinations, carrying out painstakingly detailed observations, and producing thousands of pages of notes on those observations and his ideas for explaining them.","3":"It's inconceivable that his breakthrough would have occurred if he'd tried to rush it.","4":"Consider, for example, Charles Darwin's theory of evolution, which had a protracted evolution of its own.","5":"Truly breakthrough ideas rarely hatch overnight."},"correct_sequence":["5","4","2","3"],"odd_sentence":"1","odd_sentence_error":"introduces_new_concept","odd_sentence_reason":"Refers to 'new ideas' broadly — not to 'breakthrough ideas' which is the specific focus of the paragraph. The paragraph is about Darwin's specific breakthrough, not about unstructured time for developing new ideas generally.","wrong_pick_errors":{"2":"missed_mandatory_pair","3":"missed_pronoun_link","4":"missed_mandatory_pair","5":"misread_scope"},"difficulty":"hard","explanation":"It's easy to note that there is tonal consistency in 3, 4, and 5 – refer to 'tried to rush it', 'Darwin's theory of evolution, which had a protracted evolution of its own', and 'ideas rarely hatch overnight.' Also, 42 makes a mandatory pair – 'protracted evolution' mentioned in 4 is clearly described in 2. Also, 5 and 3 form a part of our paragraph – 'breakthrough' being decisive. Since 'his' in 3 refers to Darwin, 4,2,3 make a triplet. 3 cannot begin the paragraph, hence the paragraph is 5,4,2,3. The odd one out is 1 – it refers to 'new ideas', not 'breakthrough idea Ans: (1)","time_benchmark_seconds":150,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Each of the following questions presents 5 statements of which 4, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_020","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"five_sentences","sentences":{"1":"In retrospect, that controversy generated more heat than light, and confusion is still widespread.","2":"This confusion has profound functional implications; not only is IQ a recognizably consistent measure, it also independently predicts real outcomes such as academic grades, income, social mobility, happiness, marital stability and satisfaction, general health, longevity, reduced risk of accidents, reduced risk of drug addiction, and reduced likelihood of committing violence and crimes.","3":"The high heritability of intelligence has captured the attention of many researchers across diverse disciplines, and has spurred a century-long debate which still endures.","4":"Even within the field of Psychology, many appear unclear about the implications of the heritability of IQ, and are unaware of the impact of gene-environment interplay on estimates of heritability.","5":"Even though the heritability of intelligence is high (at least in some populations), evidence from multiple lines of research suggests that variation in intelligence is greatly affected by normal environmental variation; in other words, one can say that IQ has a high heritability and a high malleability."},"correct_sequence":["3","1","4","2"],"odd_sentence":"5","odd_sentence_error":"contradicts_paragraph","odd_sentence_reason":"States that the heritability of intelligence is high — directly contradicts sentences 3 and 4 which state that many appear unclear about the implications of heritability and the debate still endures.","wrong_pick_errors":{"1":"missed_mandatory_pair","2":"missed_mandatory_pair","3":"misread_scope","4":"missed_pronoun_link"},"difficulty":"hard","explanation":"There are significant clues through the paragraph, 1 mentions (in retrospect' and 'controversy', which connects to the past 'century-long' and 'debate' in 3, making 3, 1 a mandatory pair. Moreover, 2 states 'this confusion', which can only link to 4, refer to 'many appear unclear', making 4, 2 a mandatory pair. 1 cannot begin the paragraph, given 'that' at the beginning of the sentence. Thus, the paragraph is 3, 1, 4, 2. And the odd one out is 5. It directly contradicts 3 – 'heritability of intelligence is high' in 5 contradicts 3 and 4 – 'many appear unclear about the implications of the heritability of IQ'. Ans: (5)","time_benchmark_seconds":150,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Each of the following questions presents 5 statements of which 4, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_021","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"five_sentences","sentences":{"1":"He formulated his conclusions drawn from the lessons of history in his famous 'Speech to the academic youth' held at the University of Zurich in 1946: 'There is a remedy which ... would in a few years make all Europe ... free and ... happy. It is to re-create the European family, or as much of it as we can, and to provide it with a structure under which it can dwell in peace, in safety and in freedom. We must build a kind of United States of Europe.'","2":"Following the Second World War, he was convinced that only a united Europe could guarantee peace.","3":"Winston Churchill, a former army officer, war reporter and British Prime Minister (1940-45 and 1951-55), was one of the first to call for the creation of a 'United States of Europe'.","4":"Winston Churchill also made a name for himself as a painter and writer: in 1953 he was awarded the Nobel Prize for Literature.","5":"His aim was to eliminate the European ills of nationalism and war-mongering once and for all."},"correct_sequence":["3","2","5","1"],"odd_sentence":"4","odd_sentence_error":"irrelevant_detail","odd_sentence_reason":"Mentions Churchill as a painter and Nobel Prize winner — an irrelevant biographical detail that makes no reference to the United States of Europe agenda which is the focus of the paragraph.","wrong_pick_errors":{"1":"missed_pronoun_link","2":"missed_pronoun_link","3":"misread_scope","5":"missed_pronoun_link"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"The paragraph begins with 3, since 1, 2, and 5 make pronoun references – 'he', 'he', and 'his' respectively; 4 mentions 'also'. 2 provides context for 3 – 'following the Second World War'. 5 matches the tone of 2 – refer to 'once and for all', in 5 and 'only a united Europe' in 2. Since 5 is thus decisive, 1 provides the 'conclusions'. The paragraph is 3,2,5,1, and the odd one out is 4 which makes no reference to the agenda in the United States of Europe. Ans: (4)","time_benchmark_seconds":120,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Each of the following questions presents 5 statements of which 4, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_022","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"five_sentences","sentences":{"1":"Composing in this way is known as 'tonal music' and it provides structure and creates complementary tones that blend well together.","2":"When creating a piece of music, composers have certain frameworks in which to develop their ideas – they'll select particular keys and will use the tonic (the first note of the scale in any key) as the centre point from which to create melodies and harmonies to help flesh out their piece, for example.","3":"Compositions in this style may sound a bit chaotic or even downright weird compared with traditional tonal compositions, but it still takes skill to get it 'right'.","4":"However, atonal music rips up this set of 'rules', taking a completely different approach, where there are no discernible keys or typical harmonies.","5":"The atonal style of music took off in the early 20th century, with Arnold Schoenberg at the forefront of the genre."},"correct_sequence":["2","1","4","3"],"odd_sentence":"5","odd_sentence_error":"irrelevant_detail","odd_sentence_reason":"Provides historical context about when atonal music took off and names Schoenberg — can perhaps form part of a larger context but does not fit into the specific chain about tonal vs atonal composition.","wrong_pick_errors":{"1":"missed_mandatory_pair","2":"misread_scope","3":"missed_mandatory_pair","4":"missed_connector_link"},"difficulty":"hard","explanation":"The paragraph is full of references – 1 mentions 'composing in this way', to create 'tones that blend well together', which can only refer to 2 - 'using the tonic (the first note of the scale in any key) as the centre point from which to create melodies and harmonies'. 3 notes that 'compositions in this style may sound a bit chaotic', which can only refer to 4 - 'atonal music … where there are no discernible keys or typical harmonies'. We have two pairs 2,1 and 4, 3. Moreover, 4 mentions 'taking a completely different approach (from what is stated in 2)', hence the paragraph is 2,1,4,3. 5 is the odd one out as it can perhaps form part of the larger context of the paragraph. Ans: (5)","time_benchmark_seconds":150,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Each of the following questions presents 5 statements of which 4, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_023","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"five_sentences","sentences":{"1":"In this way, digital communication can be seen as a distinct form of language with unique features and conventions that are specific to the digital environment.","2":"Emojis seem to affect the perceived emotional intensity of a message and accentuate its perceived valence by acting as nonverbal cues in digital communication.","3":"One of the most notable changes brought about by technology is the emergence of 'text-speak,' which is a form of language that is characterized by the use of shorthand, abbreviations, and symbols in text-based communication.","4":"It is not a replacement for spoken or written language, but rather, it is an additional way of communicating and conveying meaning, which is a skill that emerged in humans as a response to a new and all-encompassing technological environment with its constraints and possibilities.","5":"Technology has had a significant impact on language in recent years, giving rise to new forms of communication and new ways of using language."},"correct_sequence":["5","3","1","4"],"odd_sentence":"2","odd_sentence_error":"introduces_new_concept","odd_sentence_reason":"Refers specifically to emojis — not to digital communication at large which is the focus of the paragraph.","wrong_pick_errors":{"1":"missed_connector_link","3":"missed_mandatory_pair","4":"missed_pronoun_link","5":"misread_scope"},"difficulty":"hard","explanation":"1 begins with 'in this way' and 4 begins with the pronoun \\"it\\" hence both cannot begin the paragraph. 2 can open the paragraph. However, 3 begins by noting 'one of the … changes brought about by technology … language', and 5 states that 'technology has had a significant impact on language in recent years', thus 5 and 3 form a logical pair. Now 'it' in 4 can refer either to 'text-speak' in 3 or to 'digital communication' in 1, 1 is more likely, since 4 refers to spoken communication and written communication. Now we know that 'in this way' in 1 refers to 'use of shorthand, abbreviations, and symbols in text-based communication' in 3, which is referred to as 'a distinct form of language with unique features and conventions' in 1. The paragraph is 5,3,1,4. The odd one out is 2, since it refers to emojis, and not to digital communication at large. Ans: (2)","time_benchmark_seconds":150,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Each of the following questions presents 5 statements of which 4, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_024","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"five_sentences","sentences":{"1":"In the words of Antonsen and Jørgensen, the borders between the public and private sector have become blurred.","2":"This state of hybridity thus means that organizations combine and balance between potentially conflicting interests such as profit and social benefit, or democratic transparency and trade secrets, and they typically also combine different governance forms such as bureaucratic rule and professional autonomy.","3":"This statement resonates with key arguments of several prominent social theorists of late, who have conceptualized current 'late modern' or 'post-modern' society as a state of boundarylessness or at least increasingly permeable boundaries between previously clear institutions and entities such as the nation state, nuclear family, sectors of society, and class.","4":"Many studies have acknowledged organizations operating in between of societal sectors, most obviously public-private enterprises (PPPs) and similar entities that have the legal form and structure of e.g. a private company but fulfill a public service.","5":"In studies of organizations, the possible first use of the concept of hybridity was by the American journalist and foreign correspondent Marquis Childs, who studied Swedish society in the 1930s and highlighted its corporativist structure with close bonds between state and enterprise, dubbing the Swedish model a 'hybrid economic structure'."},"correct_sequence":["4","2","1","3"],"odd_sentence":"5","odd_sentence_error":"introduces_new_concept","odd_sentence_reason":"Mentions a corporativist structure with close bonds between state and enterprise — this cannot be found elsewhere in the passage which refers to borderless public-private enterprises, not to corporativism.","wrong_pick_errors":{"1":"missed_mandatory_pair","2":"missed_mandatory_pair","3":"missed_mandatory_pair","4":"misread_scope"},"difficulty":"hard","explanation":"2 begins with 'this state of hybridity', and 3 begins with 'this statement'. What do these refer to? 2 'this state of hybridity' refers to 4 - 'entities that have the legal form and structure of e.g. a private company but fulfill a public service'. 3 'this statement' refers to 1 - the words of Antonsen and Jørgensen, that 'the borders between the public and private sector have become blurred'. Thus, we have two pairs, 4, 2 and 1,3. 4 gains precedence over 1, as 4,2 provides context – 'public-private enterprises' for the 'state of boundarylessness' in 1, 3. The paragraph is 4, 2, 1, 3. And the odd one out is 5. It mentions a 'corporativist structure with close bonds between state and enterprise', which cannot be found elsewhere in the passage, the passage as such refers to borderless PPEs. Ans: (5)","time_benchmark_seconds":150,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Each of the following questions presents 5 statements of which 4, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_025","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"five_sentences","sentences":{"1":"The 'Golden Age of Comics' began during America's Great Depression and lasted through World War II, ending in the mid-1950s with the passage of strict censorship regulations.","2":"Comics are not just visual: creators push their stories forward through text — speech balloons, thought clouds, and narrative boxes — which are identified and transcribed using optical character recognition (OCR).","3":"In contrast to the long, world-building story arcs popular in later eras, Golden Age comics tend to be small and self-contained; a single book usually contains multiple different stories sharing a common theme (e.g., crime or mystery).","4":"The Digital Comics Museum (DCM) hosts user-uploaded scans of many comics by lesser-known Golden Age publishers that are now in the public domain due to copyright expiration.","5":"While the best-selling Golden Age comics tell of American superheroes triumphing over German and Japanese villains, a variety of other genres (such as romance, humor, and horror) also enjoyed popularity."},"correct_sequence":["1","3","5","4"],"odd_sentence":"2","odd_sentence_error":"introduces_new_concept","odd_sentence_reason":"Gives a general description of comics as a medium — not specific to the Golden Age context. The paragraph focuses entirely on Golden Age comics characteristics, not on the general nature of comics.","wrong_pick_errors":{"1":"misread_scope","3":"missed_thematic_link","4":"missed_thematic_link","5":"missed_thematic_link"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"Statement 2 is unlikely to begin the paragraph, as we don't know which comics are being discussed. 3 and 5 refer to Golden Age comics, and 4 refers to Golden Age comics publishers but the same is actually introduced in 1 as the \\"Golden Age of Comics\\". Given that 1 begins the paragraph, 3 follows chronologically – 'began during America's Great Depression and lasted through World War II, ending in the mid-1950s' in 1 followed by 'in contrast to the long, world-building story arcs popular in later eras'. Since 3 mentions that Golden Age comics contain multiple different stories sharing a common theme, 5 states what these 'stories' in 3 are about – 'American superheroes'. 4 contrasts 5 – 5 mentions 'best-selling Golden Age comics', and 4 mentions 'comics by lesser-known Golden Age publishers'.\\nThe paragraph is 1354, and the odd one out is 2, which gives a general description of comics. Ans: (2)","time_benchmark_seconds":120,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Each of the following questions presents 5 statements of which 4, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_026","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"five_sentences","sentences":{"1":"Compassion in World Farming's biggest fear is that gene-editing animals to make them more resistant to diseases will mean that the industry will not be motivated to deal with the conditions that lead to the animals getting ill in the first place - such as crowded, unsanitary conditions.","2":"GM crops were publicly trampled by protestors who saw this as a technology that they didn't need, want or consider safe.","3":"Genetic modification was rejected by many consumers in the UK, the European Union and other countries 30 years ago because of its perceived unnaturalness.","4":"Gene editing seems to be a more palatable version of GM to some, arriving at a time when the debate is less polarized, the need for environmental solutions is even more urgent and there seems to be a greater readiness for some scientists and campaigners to see each other's perspectives.","5":"At the same time, scientists were angry and upset that what they believed to be their world-saving technology was being destroyed by, in their view, a wave of anti-scientific hysteria fuelled by the media."},"correct_sequence":["3","2","5","4"],"odd_sentence":"1","odd_sentence_error":"introduces_new_concept","odd_sentence_reason":"Raises concerns regarding gene-editing and animal welfare conditions — this concern is not found elsewhere in the passage which focuses on the public and scientific reception of GM and gene editing.","wrong_pick_errors":{"2":"missed_mandatory_pair","3":"misread_scope","4":"missed_thematic_link","5":"missed_connector_link"},"difficulty":"hard","explanation":"Neither 1 nor 2/4 can begin the paragraph, because 'gene-editing' in 1 is introduced in 4, and 'GM' in 2/4 is introduced in 3 – genetic modification. 5 is continuing a train of thought 'at the same time', hence it cannot open the paragraph either. Thus, 3 opens the paragraph. 'GM … publicly trampled by protestors' in 2 follows 'genetic modification was rejected' in 3. 5 looks at the other side of the argument 'at the same time, scientists were angry and upset', and 4 finds common ground for the protestors and the scientists in question. The paragraph is 3254. 1 is the odd one out, because it raises concerns regarding gene-editing that is not found elsewhere in the passage. 4 presents a neutral version of gene-editing. Ans: (1)","time_benchmark_seconds":150,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Each of the following questions presents 5 statements of which 4, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_027","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"five_sentences","sentences":{"1":"Increasingly, the Global South became synonymous with the Group of 77, a collection of postcolonial and developing countries that united in 1964 to jointly advocate for their collective economic interests and to enhance their negotiating capacity at the UN.","2":"Following the end of the Cold War, the term 'Third World' fell gradually out of favor, both because the Second World itself had ceased to exist and because it seemed pejorative, connoting a group of backward and unstable nations mired in poverty.","3":"Today the members of the G77, now 134 countries strong, regularly refer to themselves as the Global South, and the UN has launched multiple bodies and initiatives to respond to their needs and aspirations, including a UN Office for South-South Cooperation.","4":"By comparison, the 'Global South' offered a more neutral and appealing label.","5":"To avoid repeating past mistakes, Western policymakers should beware reifying the Global South, as if it were a single entity, and instead tailor strategies of engagement to specific countries, not least when it comes to so-called pivotal (or swing) states such as Brazil, India, Indonesia, South Africa, or Türkiye."},"correct_sequence":["2","4","1","3"],"odd_sentence":"5","odd_sentence_error":"wrong_scope","odd_sentence_reason":"Sounds a note of caution about Western policymakers treating the Global South as a single entity — no concern of this kind is raised elsewhere in the paragraph which traces the evolution of the term 'Global South'.","wrong_pick_errors":{"1":"missed_mandatory_pair","2":"misread_scope","3":"missed_mandatory_pair","4":"missed_connector_link"},"difficulty":"hard","explanation":"Since the \\"Global South\\" is introduced in 4, 1, 3, and 5, which mention the Global South cannot begin the paragraph. Now 4 itself is a 'comparison', to 2, which is the only contender now to begin the paragraph. Given 24, we know that 'G77' in 3 is introduced in 1, 'the Group of 77'. Hence, 13 makes a mandatory pair, and 2413 is our paragraph. 5 is then the odd one out. 5 sounds a note of caution, but no concern about the Global South is raised elsewhere in the paragraph jumble. Ans: (5)","time_benchmark_seconds":150,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Each of the following questions presents 5 statements of which 4, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_028","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"five_sentences","sentences":{"1":"It is important to note that while it is relatively cheap to produce biological weapon agents in large quantities, sophisticated weapons are slightly more difficult to develop and produce.","2":"In addition, storing biological weapon agents requires much effort, due to the quick decay of many of these sorts of agents.","3":"Not surprisingly, biological weapons have long since become known as the poor man's atom bomb.","4":"For example, when a missile is flying it gets very hot, and biological agents are killed; therefore, the missile has to be fitted with a cooling system.","5":"However, as far as weapons of mass destruction are concerned, biological weapons are relatively cheap to develop and produce."},"correct_sequence":["1","4","2","5"],"odd_sentence":"3","odd_sentence_error":"unsubstantiated","odd_sentence_reason":"States that biological weapons are known as the poor man's atom bomb — this claim is not substantiated with evidence in the passage which discusses the technicalities of biological weapons, not their implications.","wrong_pick_errors":{"1":"misread_scope","2":"missed_connector_link","4":"missed_connector_link","5":"missed_connector_link"},"difficulty":"hard","explanation":"Only 1 can begin the paragraph, as 2, 3, 4, and 5 have problematic elements – 'in addition', not surprisingly', 'for example', and 'however' respectively, which lack precedence. 1 mentions 'sophisticated weapons are slightly more difficult to develop and produce', and 4 follows with technical problems – 'missile has to be fitted with a cooling system'. 2 add to the problems in 4 – 'storing biological weapon agents requires much effort'. 5 states that despite these technical problems, biological weapons do pose a threat to humanity as weapons of mass destruction. The paragraph is 1,4,2,5. 3 is not substantiated with evidence in the passage – why biological weapons should be known as the poor man's atom bomb is not clear, since the passage discusses the technicalities of biological weapons, not their implications. Ans: (3)","time_benchmark_seconds":150,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Each of the following questions presents 5 statements of which 4, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_029","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"five_sentences","sentences":{"1":"Kate Chopin has, as Per Seyersted suggests in his critical biography, '...a daring and a vision all her own...'.","2":"The French male form and style she adopts from Maupassant are adapted to suit her own purpose, to explore a position unique to women; she exerts literary individuality and originality and, ultimately, speaks in a truly feminist voice.","3":"Chopin's uniqueness as a woman writer, at its best, speaks out well beyond the French influence and it is a voice which is strikingly female.","4":"That Chopin in some way 'bought into' the French male literary tradition which presented itself to her in the form of Maupassant's short fiction is undeniable.","5":"French short story writer Guy de Maupassant undoubtedly provided inspiration for her own creative spirit, his themes and techniques being clearly evident in her work but '...with her independent spirit and her personal views she stood entirely on her own'."},"correct_sequence":["1","5","3","2"],"odd_sentence":"4","odd_sentence_error":"contradicts_paragraph","odd_sentence_reason":"Mentions that Chopin 'bought into' the French male literary tradition — this criticism of Chopin's dependence on the French tradition is not evident elsewhere in the paragraph which celebrates her independent feminist voice.","wrong_pick_errors":{"1":"misread_scope","2":"missed_thematic_link","3":"missed_thematic_link","5":"missed_mandatory_pair"},"difficulty":"hard","explanation":"Only 1 can begin the paragraph. 2, 3, 4, and 5 mention 'she', 'Chopin(not full name)', 'Chopin(not full name)', and 'her' respectively, thus lacking precedence. Given 1 – 'a vision all her own', 5 clearly follows 'she stood entirely on her own'. 5 also mentions 'French short story writer Guy de Maupassant … provid(ing) inspiration for her own creative spirit, … themes and techniques,' however, as 5 attests to Chopin's 'independent spirit' – 3 supports this observation with '(Chopin) speaking out well beyond the French influence'. 2 substantiates this observation 'French male form and style she adopts from Maupassant are adapted to suit her own purpose', and closes the paragraph with Chopin 'ultimately, speaking in a truly feminist voice'. The paragraph, thus, is 1,5,3,2. 4 mentions that Chopin ''bought into' the French male literary tradition', but this criticism is not evident elsewhere in the paragraph jumble. Ans: (4)","time_benchmark_seconds":150,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Each of the following questions presents 5 statements of which 4, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_030","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"five_sentences","sentences":{"1":"And this intense enforcement will affect not only the big tech companies but also the smaller ones that hope to be acquired by them.","2":"In another deal where the parties had a vertical relationship, Amazon's proposed acquisition of iRobot, the parties abandoned their deal in January 2024 due to a statement of objection and probable challenge by the European Commission (EC).","3":"If selling companies are thinking about selling to a big tech player, they need to carefully assess the risks of facing a prolonged investigation and a possible challenge.","4":"Meta and Microsoft managed to close significant deals despite antitrust opposition in 2023, but there is no sign that global antitrust regulators will ease up on their scrutiny of acquisitions by big tech companies.","5":"Public reports by the largest tech companies indicate that their acquisition spending dropped sharply in 2023."},"correct_sequence":["4","1","5","3"],"odd_sentence":"2","odd_sentence_error":"introduces_new_concept","odd_sentence_reason":"Mentions the European Commission specifically — while sentences 4, 1, 5, 3 refer to cases handled by global antitrust regulators, sentence 2 introduces the EC which does not fit into the given context of global regulators.","wrong_pick_errors":{"1":"missed_mandatory_pair","3":"missed_thematic_link","4":"misread_scope","5":"missed_thematic_link"},"difficulty":"hard","explanation":"Statements 1 and 2 cannot begin the paragraph, for they have problematic references – 'and' in 1, and 'another' in 2. Statement 3 is not framed in context – we don't know what 'selling companies' mean, to be precise. Statements 4 or 5 can begin the paragraph, on paper, at least. Now 4, 1 makes a mandatory pair, we know what 'and' in 1 refers to now - global antitrust regulators will (not) ease up' in 4 followed by 'and this intense enforcement' in 1. Now 1 mentions 'will affect not only the big tech companies', which is supported by evidence in 5 'largest tech companies … their acquisition spending dropped sharply', and 1 also mentions 'this intense enforcement will (also) affect the smaller (companies)', which leads to the warning in 3 – '(smaller) companies thinking about selling to a big tech player, they need to carefully assess the risks of facing a prolonged investigation and a possible challenge.' Thus, the paragraph is 4,1,5,3. 2 is the odd one out, while 4,1,5,3 refer to cases handled by global antitrust regulators, 2 mentions the EC, which does not fit into the given context. Ans: (2)","time_benchmark_seconds":150,"instructions":"Directions for questions 1 to 30: Each of the following questions presents 5 statements of which 4, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_031","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"four_sentences","sentences":{"1":"Most important, former smokers have to rediscover that it is possible to enjoy life without cigarettes, although the yearning may never die completely.","2":"Today, 19 percent of American adults smoke, down from more than 42 percent half a century ago, when Luther Terry, the United States surgeon general, formed a committee to produce the first official report on the health effects of smoking.","3":"Now, however, the decline in adult smoking has stalled despite the economic downturn and the soaring price of cigarettes.","4":"Ever-increasing restrictions on where people can smoke have helped to swell the ranks of former smokers."},"correct_sequence":["2","4","3"],"odd_sentence":"1","odd_sentence_error":"shifts_focus","odd_sentence_reason":"Talks about factors that lure once-smokers back into the habit — a different focus from the paragraph which discusses trends in the number of smokers over time.","wrong_pick_errors":{"2":"misread_scope","3":"missed_connector_link","4":"missed_thematic_link"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"By going through the sentences, we understand that the paragraph talks about the long term decrease in smokers in America, a decrease which has slowed down recently.\\nSentence 2 is the first sentence because it introduces the concept – the decrease in the number of smokers over a period introduced here.\\nSentence 4 is the second sentence because it explains the cause for the observed decrease during the said period.\\nSentence 3 is the third sentence because it brings out the contrast between the continuous decrease during the stated period and the present slowing down of the decrease. This contrast is brought by using however in this sentence.\\nSentence 1 is odd in the context because it gives information about factors that lure the once-smokers into the habit again. This is not related to the trends being discussed.\\nThus, the odd sentence is Sentence 1. Ans : (1)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 31 to 40: Each of the following questions presents 4 statements of which 3, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_032","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"four_sentences","sentences":{"1":"In recent years, some research has suggested that a high-fat diet may be bad for the brain, at least in lab animals.","2":"Can exercise protect against such damage?","3":"That question may have particular relevance now, as the butter-and cream-laden holidays fast approaching have prompted several new and important studies.","4":"So if you can't walk away from the buffet table, be sure to at least take a walk afterward."},"correct_sequence":["1","2","3"],"odd_sentence":"4","odd_sentence_error":"wrong_scope","odd_sentence_reason":"Starts with 'so' but none of the sentences in the earlier part can give the reason to justify this use of 'so' — the paragraph sets up a question, not a conclusion that 'so' could follow from.","wrong_pick_errors":{"1":"misread_scope","2":"missed_mandatory_pair","3":"missed_mandatory_pair"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"The main idea of the paragraph is that a high-fat diet may be bad for the brain. The paragraph further posits how exercise can act as a protection against such brain damage.\\nSentence 1 is the first sentence because it introduces the topic by mentioning the recent research.\\nSentence 2 is the second sentence because it raises a question about the damage mentioned in the earlier sentence.\\nSentence 3 is the third sentence because it uses the question to refer to the question raised in the earlier sentence.\\nThus, the correct sequence is 1, 2 and 3.\\nSentence 4 is the odd sentence because it starts with so, but none of the sentences in the earlier part can give the reason to justify this use of the conjunction so.\\nThus, the odd sentence is Sentence 4. Ans : (4)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 31 to 40: Each of the following questions presents 4 statements of which 3, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_033","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"four_sentences","sentences":{"1":"The most serene relationships are those in which the power structure is accepted unquestioningly by both partners in the dyad, and both can therefore be relatively true to their respective selves and each other within the framework of this acceptance.","2":"Looking around, one can see that in most dyadic relationships (those involving two people), there is the tacit, often explicit, assumption, that one of the two has a casting vote.","3":"Whether between parent and child, man and woman, boss and subordinate, teacher and student, sibling and sibling, friend and friend or service provider and service recipient, most fallouts take place when one doesn't recognise or respect the authority of the other, or worse, attempts to reverse the power balance in the equation.","4":"So for millennia, in our country certainly, patriarchy has been accepted as an incontrovertible bedrock of cultural existence, except in certain pockets, where matriarchy ruled the roost."},"correct_sequence":["2","3","1"],"odd_sentence":"4","odd_sentence_error":"introduces_new_concept","odd_sentence_reason":"Patriarchy and matriarchy are not relevant to the dyadic relationship described in the paragraph — the paragraph focuses on power structures within two-person relationships in general, not on gender-based social structures.","wrong_pick_errors":{"1":"missed_thematic_link","2":"misread_scope","3":"missed_thematic_link"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"The paragraph talks about the power structure in dyadic relationships and serene relationships developed between the two participants.\\nSentence 2 is the first sentence because it defines and introduces the dyadic relationship which is described in the remaining sentences.\\nSentence 3 is the second sentence because it lists out different persons between which the dyadic relationship exists and also mentions the fall-outs in this relation.\\nSentence 1 is the third sentence because it explains where the relationship remains serene and strong.\\nThus, the logical sequence is 2, 3, 1.\\nSentence 4 is the odd sentence because patriarchy and matriarchy mentioned in this option are not relevant to the dyadic relationship mentioned in the paragraph.\\nThus, the correct option is Sentence 4. Ans : (4)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 31 to 40: Each of the following questions presents 4 statements of which 3, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_034","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"four_sentences","sentences":{"1":"The study by Liverpool John Moores University is the first to suggest that a simple exercise programme has an effect similar to that of the stem cells, when they are cajoled into producing new tissues through special shots.","2":"Regular and vigorous exercise can activate dormant stem cells in the heart, which heal the damage caused by a heart attack, says a new study.","3":"People suffering from even low, subclinical levels of psychological stress are at an increased risk of death from external causes and cardiovascular diseases.","4":"Strenuous exercises include 30 minutes of running or cycling daily, enough to work up a sweat, the European Heart Journal reports."},"correct_sequence":["2","1","4"],"odd_sentence":"3","odd_sentence_error":"introduces_new_concept","odd_sentence_reason":"Talks about stress and its resulting in cardiac problems — the paragraph focuses on exercise as a remedy for cardiac problems via stem cell activation, not on stress as a cause.","wrong_pick_errors":{"1":"missed_mandatory_pair","2":"misread_scope","4":"missed_thematic_link"},"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"When we go through the sentences, we understand that the paragraph is about the usefulness of exercise to combat cardiac problems.\\nSentence 2 is the first sentence because it introduces the topic by mentioning a study, which is generally used (because of the indefinite article) in an introductory sentence.\\nSentence 1 is the second sentence because it uses the noun the study to refer to the study presented in the earlier sentence.\\nSentence 4 is the last sentence because it gives further information about the usefulness of exercise.\\nThus, the correct word order is 2,1 , 4.\\nSentence 3 is odd because it talks about stress and its resulting in cardiac problems, a topic which is not related to the main idea of the paragraph.\\nThus, the correct option is Sentence 4. Ans : (3)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 31 to 40: Each of the following questions presents 4 statements of which 3, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_035","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"four_sentences","sentences":{"1":"Cancer cells thrive in low-oxygen conditions, reverting to an earlier, albeit less efficient, form of metabolism known as fermentation.","2":"Unfortunately, the emphasis on cancer cells as defective loose cannons is at odds with the stubborn way they outwit both the body's defences and the physician's armoury.","3":"Medical science treats cancer as a disease in which rogue cells proliferate uncontrollably, running amok around the body.","4":"Therapy focuses on killing the cancer before it kills the host."},"correct_sequence":["3","4","2"],"odd_sentence":"1","odd_sentence_error":"introduces_new_concept","odd_sentence_reason":"Talks about cancer cells thriving in low-oxygen conditions through fermentation — information not at all related to the treatment approach and its limitations which is the main idea of the paragraph.","wrong_pick_errors":{"2":"missed_connector_link","3":"misread_scope","4":"missed_mandatory_pair"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"The paragraph is about the nature of cancer, the nature of the treatment and a shortcoming of that treatment.\\nSentence 3 is the first sentence because it introduces the nature of cancer along with the introduction of the treatment.\\nSentence 4 is the second sentence because it brings out the first step in the treatment of cancer; it might be useful to note the use of definite article the before the noun cancer used in this sentence.\\nSentence 2 is the third sentence because it brings out the flaw in the treatment (by using the word unfortunately) and concludes the paragraph aptly.\\nThus, the correct sequence is 3, 4, 2.\\nSentence 1 is odd because the information in this option is not at all related to the treatment of cancer.\\nThus, the best option is Sentence 1. Ans : (1)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 31 to 40: Each of the following questions presents 4 statements of which 3, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_036","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"four_sentences","sentences":{"1":"Though public mistrust of politics is at unprecedented levels, successive attempts at reform, good and bad alike have grazed or run onto the rocks of public indifference too often.","2":"Nobody whose vision of a better Britain includes a stronger democracy, positive human rights, more vigorous local institutions and a less deferential social order should take satisfaction from the police and crime commissioner election results last week.","3":"The public may say they want change, but conservatives believe the public is more at ease with the old order.","4":"The inadequacies of the police and crime commissioner system are many, but the low turnout and the resulting weak mandate for those elected have written another troubling chapter in the saga of failed British democratic and constitutional reform."},"correct_sequence":["2","4","1"],"odd_sentence":"3","odd_sentence_error":"introduces_new_concept","odd_sentence_reason":"Mentions something irrelevant to the main context — the conservative view about the public being at ease with the old order is not directly connected to the paragraph's focus on public indifference towards the police and crime commissioner election.","wrong_pick_errors":{"1":"missed_thematic_link","2":"misread_scope","4":"missed_connector_link"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"Going through these rather lengthy and demanding sentences, we understand that the paragraph is about public indifference towards the election for the Police and Crime Commissioner.\\nSentence 2 is the first sentence because it broadly says that no one is happy with the election; this way, it introduces the topic effectively.\\nSentence 4 is the second sentence because it explains why the election is unsatisfactory.\\nSentence 1 is the concluding sentence because it gives further generalization (about public indifference) basing on the earlier sentences.\\nThus, the correct sequence is 2, 4,1.\\nSentence 3 is odd because it mentions something irrelevant to the main context.\\nThus, the correct option is Sentence 3. Ans : (3)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 31 to 40: Each of the following questions presents 4 statements of which 3, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_037","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"four_sentences","sentences":{"1":"Every day after school I would head for the restaurant and work until ten.","2":"Saturdays I worked from two until eleven.","3":"My first job taught me discipline, responsibility and brought me a level of personal satisfaction few of my friends had experienced.","4":"At that age it was tough going to work and watching my friends run off to swim or play."},"correct_sequence":["1","2","4"],"odd_sentence":"3","odd_sentence_error":"introduces_new_concept","odd_sentence_reason":"Though it seems related to the whole context, we do not have any idea whether the job described is the first job or not — the paragraph describes a specific job during school days without establishing it as a first job.","wrong_pick_errors":{"1":"misread_scope","2":"missed_thematic_link","4":"missed_thematic_link"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"When we go through the sentences, we understand that the paragraph is the narration of description of job during school days.\\nSentence 1 is the first sentence because it begins the narration with the description of the job.\\nSentence 2 is the second sentence because it continues the description by giving additional information about the timings of his job.\\nSentence 4 is the last sentence because it gives a general comment about the difficulty of the job the narrator faced during that period.\\nThus, the correct sequence is 1, 2, 4.\\nSentence 3 is the odd sentence because, though it seems to be very much related to the whole context, we do not have any idea whether the job described is the first job or not.\\nThus, the odd sentence is Sentence 3. Ans : (3)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 31 to 40: Each of the following questions presents 4 statements of which 3, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_038","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"four_sentences","sentences":{"1":"If you want to strengthen the muscles of your arm to achieve more, you must train them.","2":"If you want to toughen up your leg muscles, you must first exert them.","3":"If you want to live a more peaceful life, you must think more peaceful thoughts.","4":"Similarly, your mind will do wonderful things for you if you only let it."},"correct_sequence":["1","2","4"],"odd_sentence":"3","odd_sentence_error":"shifts_focus","odd_sentence_reason":"Talks about living a more peaceful life through peaceful thoughts — does not talk about training a part of the body to achieve results, which is the consistent theme of the paragraph.","wrong_pick_errors":{"1":"missed_thematic_link","2":"missed_thematic_link","4":"missed_connector_link"},"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"Going through the sentences, we understand that the paragraph is about using mind or a part of the body to achieve desired results.\\nSentence 1 is a possible choice for the first sentence because of its generic nature.\\nSentence 2 is a possible choice for the second sentence because it exemplifies one factor discussed in the paragraph.\\nSentence 4 is the last sentence because the use of similarly requires a similar situation before it.\\nIt is to be noted that Sentence 1 and Sentence 2 can be the beginning sentences in either order. Thus, the correct sequence is 1, 2, 4 or 2, 1, 4.\\nSentence 3 is the odd sentence because it does not talk about training; in fact, it talks about 'living a peaceful life', which is out of context.\\nThus, the correct choice is Sentence 3. Ans : (3)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 31 to 40: Each of the following questions presents 4 statements of which 3, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_039","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"four_sentences","sentences":{"1":"Darkness will envelop the strangler figs and pencil cedars of the forests below and engulf the angelfish and wrasse on the Great Barrier Reef.","2":"On Tuesday, the sky above Queensland's reefs and rainforests will undergo a brief but dramatic transformation.","3":"The landscape that lies below the eclipse's path is one of the most remarkable on the planet: a juxtaposition of rainforest and a vast expanse of gleaming coral.","4":"Just after sunrise, a black mark will appear at the sun's edge and expand to cover its entire disc."},"correct_sequence":["2","4","1"],"odd_sentence":"3","odd_sentence_error":"shifts_focus","odd_sentence_reason":"Focuses on the specialty of the landscape below the eclipse's path rather than on the effect of the eclipse on the area — the paragraph describes the eclipse and its dramatic effect, not the landscape.","wrong_pick_errors":{"1":"missed_thematic_link","2":"misread_scope","4":"missed_mandatory_pair"},"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"Going through the sentences, we understand that the paragraph describes an occurrence of a solar eclipse and its dramatic effect on the Great Barrier Reef.\\nSentence 2 introduces the topic by mentioning the date of the occurrence of the eclipse and also the overall area which is affected by the eclipse. Thus, this is the first sentence.\\nSentence 4 presents the beginning of the eclipse and its progression. Hence, this sentence logically follows the first sentence because it gives specific information about the eclipse mentioned in the sentence.\\nSentence 1 is the third and concluding sentence of the paragraph as it details the dramatic transformation mentioned in the earlier sentence.\\nThus, the proper sequence of sentences to form a coherent paragraph is 2, 4, 1.\\nSentence 3 is odd sentence because this focuses on the specialty of the landscape that lay below the eclipse's path, rather than on the effect of the eclipse on the area.\\nThus, the best option is Option 3. Ans : (3)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 31 to 40: Each of the following questions presents 4 statements of which 3, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."},{"id":"OO_E6_040","exercise":"exercise_6","type":"odd_man_out","subtype":"four_sentences","sentences":{"1":"It may no longer be just anecdotal that every third person in Kerala is a migrant worker from outside the State.","2":"When people from Kerala migrated to various parts of Asia and Africa during the days of the British Empire, they were in the forefront of struggles for workers' rights.","3":"Along with Karnataka and Tamil Nadu, Kerala is experiencing a massive influx of migrant workers into its emerging urban areas and its hinterland.","4":"High wages, and the shortage of skilled and unskilled labour due to high education levels and migration from the State to West Asian countries, make Kerala an attractive destination for workers from north, central and northeastern India."},"correct_sequence":["1","3","4"],"odd_sentence":"2","odd_sentence_error":"shifts_focus","odd_sentence_reason":"Talks about the struggle for workers' rights by Kerala employees in other parts of the world — this is not relevant to the paragraph which focuses on migrant workers coming INTO Kerala, not Keralites going out.","wrong_pick_errors":{"1":"misread_scope","3":"missed_thematic_link","4":"missed_thematic_link"},"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"The sentences talk about the prevalence of immigrant workers in Kerala and give explanation to that phenomenon.\\nSentence 1 is the first sentence because it introduces the existence of migrant workers from outside the state.\\nSentence 3 is the second sentence because it gives further description of the situation.\\nSentence 4 is the fourth sentence because it gives the reasons for the phenomenon.\\nThus, the correct sequence is 1, 3, 4.\\nSentence 2 is the odd sentence because it talks about the struggle for workers' rights by Kerala employees in other parts of the world. This is not relevant to the situation which is the focus of the paragraph.\\nThus, the odd sentence is Sentence 2. Ans : (2)","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"instructions":"Directions for questions 31 to 40: Each of the following questions presents 4 statements of which 3, when placed in appropriate order, would form a contextually complete paragraph. Pick the statement that is not part of that context and mark the number corresponding with that statement in the box provided below each question."}]`);export{e as default};
